      "why": "Pipelined_m also achieves 1 result/cycle throughput, so it can meet the 32 Msps constraint. It registers every m stages instead of every stage, which reduces FF count compared to fully pipelined for the same N, but increases latency to ceil(N/m)+2. Since the objective is to minimize luts_plus_ffs, pipelined_m with m>1 could have fewer FFs than pipelined, potentially landing on the Pareto front if the latency still meets the 0.44us p99 batch constraint. The batch constraint requires all 32 results within 0.44us of the tick. With 1 result/cycle throughput, 32 results take 32 cycles. At Fmax, 32 cycles must fit in 0.44us, so Fmax >= 32/0.44us = 72.7 MHz. Pipelined_m with m=2-4 and N=12-18 has latency ceil(N/m)+2 = 5-11 cycles, which is well below 32 cycles, so the batch latency is dominated by the 32-cycle throughput time, not the pipeline latency. This means pipelined_m is feasible and worth exploring for its lower FF count. m is limited to 2-4 to keep latency reasonable and avoid excessive area from very wide combinational blocks."
