  "rationale": "The spec has three hard constraints: throughput >= 32 Msps, max_abs_err <= 2^-12, and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44us. The throughput constraint is the most restrictive: it requires 1 result/cycle at Fmax >= 32 MHz (since 32 Msps = 32 results/us, and 1 result/cycle means Fmax >= 32 MHz). The iterative and unrolled_k families produce 1 result every N or N/k cycles, so to achieve 32 Msps they would need Fmax >= 32*N MHz or 32*N/k MHz, which is far beyond what an Artix-7 can achieve for a CORDIC datapath. Therefore, only pipelined and pipelined_m families are feasible. The accuracy constraint requires n_iter and data_width sufficient to achieve 12-bit accuracy; CORDIC residual error after N iterations is roughly 2^(-N) in the worst case, so N >= 12 is needed. data_width >= 14 ensures the output LSB (2^-(W-2)) is at most 2^-12. The batch latency constraint (0.44us for 32 results) requires Fmax >= 72.7 MHz, which is achievable for a pipelined CORDIC on Artix-7. The objective is to minimize luts_plus_ffs, so we want the smallest data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, and frac_guard that meet the accuracy constraint, and the architecture with the fewest FFs (pipelined_m with m>1 may have fewer FFs than pipelined). We search pipelined and pipelined_m with narrow ranges focused on the feasible region: data_width 14-18, n_iter 12-18, angle_guard 0-2, frac_guard 0-2. The budget is split 60/40 between pipelined and pipelined_m, with pipelined getting more budget because it is the baseline and more likely to have the best Fmax (fully pipelined has shorter critical path per stage), while pipelined_m is explored for its potential FF savings. The accuracy_bits objective (maximize) will be naturally satisfied by designs that meet the 2^-12 constraint with margin, so the primary trade-off is area vs. Fmax (which affects whether the design meets the batch latency constraint at the required Fmax)."
